<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">British Kaffraria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1847">1847</a>–<a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">British Kaffraria was a British territory between the Keiskamma and Kei rivers in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, annexed by Governor Sir Harry Smith in 1847 after the War of the Axe. Governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-williams-town">King William's Town</a> and made a separate crown colony in 1860, it was settled by Xhosa under British control and by German military settlers, and it was annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> in 1866.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/british-kaffraria.jpg" alt="British Kaffraria" width="320" height="280" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: I created the map, based partly on the source map: Eastern frontier of the Colony of the Cape of Good Hope from Algoa Bay to the … · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_Frontier,_Cape_of_Good_Hope,_ca_1835.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Kaffraria derives from the derogatory word Kaffir, used for the Black African inhabitants of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, itself from the Arabic kafir, usually translated as disbeliever. The term came to mean the Xhosa lands of what is now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a>: the western part under British rule was called British Kaffraria, and the still independent Xhosa territory to the east, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transkei">Transkei</a>, Kaffraria proper.</p><p>The area's first inhabitants were Khoisan hunter-gatherers and herders, displaced by the Bantu expansion when it crossed the Kei River. The land then came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngqika">Ngqika</a> chiefs, of the Rarabe branch of the Xhosa, ruled by Ngqika ka Mlawu from 1797 and by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandile">Sandile</a> kaNgqika from 13 November 1829.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> first seized the area after the 6th Frontier War, when Governor Sir Benjamin d'Urban annexed it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> on 10 May 1835 as Queen Adelaide Province, named after the wife of King William IV, with its seat at the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-williams-town">King William's Town</a>. The Xhosa were driven across the Kei River and magistrates were placed at the chiefs' Great Places. London condemned the annexation as uneconomical and unjust, and in December 1836 it was undone and the Cape's border returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keiskamma-river">Keiskamma River</a>.</p><p>After the 7th Frontier War, Governor Sir Harry Smith annexed the territory again on 17 December 1847, re-occupied the abandoned forts and moved imperial troops in. A military officer as chief commissioner governed it, with assistant commissioners acting as magistrates. Xhosa chiefs had to acknowledge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> and kept only limited authority, their decisions open to reversal. During the Mlanjeni War of 1850–1853, Smith tried to unseat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandile">Sandile</a>, paramount chief of the Rharhabe tribes.</p><p>German emigrants arrived in 1858–1859. On 7 March 1860, in the wake of the Xhosa cattle-killing and the famine that followed it, the territory became a separate crown colony under Lieutenant Governor Colonel John Maclean, but economic problems threatened to bankrupt it, and it was re-incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> on 17 April 1866.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>From 1853 to 1866 the territory used the stamps of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>. Its lands correspond to the districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-williams-town">Qonce</a>, formerly King William's Town, and East London, and part of the former colony was later reconstituted by the apartheid regime as the semi-independent homeland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciskei">Ciskei</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “British Kaffraria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The War of the Axe of 1846–1847 and the long war of 1850–1853 brought the creation of <b>British Kaffraria</b> between the Keiskamma and Kei rivers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Military</a></li></ul></section>
